{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"courteous-fuchsia-swallow","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Summary\n\n`rebar` gate-verification snapshots accumulate without bound under the per-user\ntemp directory. On this host the tree reached **14,705 entries** at\n`/var/folders/8l/<darwin-user>/T/rebar-gate-snapshots/`. macOS's filesystem-events\ndaemon (`fseventsd`) indexes every one of them, and grew to **26.3 GB RSS**\n(27,568,176 KB) with sustained **>150% CPU**, exhausting swap and starving the\nmachine until applications could no longer launch.\n\n## Evidence (observed 2026-08-26)\n\n- `fseventsd` PID 9685, started Aug 18, RSS 27,568,176 KB (~26.3 GB), 146–169% CPU.\n- Swap exhausted: 53,556 MB used of 54,272 MB total (**715 MB free**) on a 128 GB host.\n- Load average peaked at **58.49** on 18 cores (6 performance cores).\n- `du -sh` over `rebar-gate-snapshots` **could not complete a walk in 10 minutes**.\n- Snapshot dir count: `ls -1 .../T/rebar-gate-snapshots | wc -l` => **14,705**.\n- Entries are content-addressed dirs plus `.caveats.json` siblings, e.g.\n  `00016b6f94470185e4cfae0bb8f95b2e9b69d51b` and\n  `00016b6f94470185e4cfae0bb8f95b2e9b69d51b.caveats.json`.\n\n## Impact\n\n- Host-wide resource starvation: GUI applications failed to launch (dock bounce,\n  no window) while swap was pinned.\n- The same temp tree was the region an unrelated runaway `rm -rf` spent ~67 minutes\n  traversing, extending that incident's blast-radius window considerably.\n- Snapshots are written by the plan-review and completion-verifier gates, so the\n  growth rate scales with gate throughput. Hosts running many parallel agents hit\n  this fastest.\n\n## Remediation applied (symptomatic only)\n\n`sudo launchctl kickstart -k system/com.apple.fseventsd` — `fseventsd` restarted\nand dropped from 26.3 GB to 11 MB. This does not address the accumulation; the\nsnapshot tree is still present and will re-drive the daemon.\n\n## Suspected cause\n\nGate snapshots are created per verification run under `TMPDIR` but nothing prunes\nthem. There appears to be no retention bound (count, age, or total size) and no\ncleanup on gate completion, so every `review-plan` / `verify-completion` run on a\nlong-lived host is additive. macOS does not reliably clear `TMPDIR` on a host with\nlong uptime (this one: 41 days).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n1. Gate-snapshot creation enforces a retention bound — by age, count, or total\n   size — with the bound configurable and a documented default.\n2. Snapshots for a completed gate run are removed when the run finishes, including\n   the `.caveats.json` sibling, on both the success and failure paths.\n3. A snapshot left behind by a crashed or killed gate run is reclaimed by a\n   subsequent run rather than leaking permanently.\n4. Retention is enforced in-process (not via an external cron or CI job), so it\n   holds on any host regardless of CI provider — see `project.portability`.\n5. A regression test asserts that N+1 gate runs past the retention bound leave at\n   most the bound's worth of snapshot directories on disk.\n6. Cleanup is safe under concurrency: a snapshot in active use by another gate run\n   is never removed (this store is written by many parallel agents).\n\n## Definition of Done\n\n- Retention logic implemented and covered by the test in AC 5.\n- Concurrency safety covered by a test exercising AC 6.\n- Operator-facing note documenting the default bound and how to tune it.\n- A documented one-off command for operators to reclaim an already-bloated tree,\n  since existing hosts will not self-heal.\n\n## Notes\n\nFiled from a live incident investigation. The `fseventsd` blowup was diagnosed\nwhile triaging a separate `rm -rf` incident on the same host; the two are\nindependent in origin but interacted badly. Retention is the rebar-side defect\nand is what this ticket tracks.","id":"a37c-d55c-72c3-439b","parent_id":"","priority":2,"repos":["rebar"],"tags":["infra","resource-leak","gates"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Unbounded gate-snapshot accumulation in TMPDIR drives fseventsd to 26GB RSS and starves the host"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787771261934028355,"uuid":"4e0096cd-0db8-4679-a584-b211d3c381af"}